VERDICT: OVERRULE
CHECKER: judge-standards
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: X1
ATTEMPT: 2
---
Read on disk: internal/services/mcpsvc/snapshot/snapshot_test.go (the actual
comment, not the checkers' paraphrases), snapshot.go:1-70 (Ensure's own doc
comment), docs/whatif-mcp-followups-2026-08-09.md §3, `gh issue view 26
--json body`, and .swarm/X-RUN-SPEC.md's X1 section + Amendments.

Written acceptance for X1 attempt 2, as stated by the lead and confirmed in
X-RUN-SPEC.md: "honest rename of the missing-file case; a genuinely-
unreadable case (source exists, read fails, error surfaced, no destination
file leaked); root-proof per D-2026-08-29e; package vet/tests green." All
four are satisfied and mutation-verified — checker-second's own report
concedes this explicitly ("technical assertions in the new test are correct
and mutation-verified"). checker-tests independently proved C1-C6 with
commands, including the root-namespace EISDIR proof and two adversarial
mutations (swallow-error, leak-a-.bak) that the new test catches. Nothing in
this is in dispute.

The sole contested point is the new test's doc comment, which cites
docs/whatif-mcp-followups-2026-08-09.md §3 for an encryption/blind-copy
rationale that §3 does not support and that arguably conflicts with Ensure's
own neighboring doc comment (snapshot.go:47-50, ciphertext being exactly
what os.ReadFile CAN read). checker-second's textual reading of §3 is
correct on its own terms — §3 ("Data-directory resolution") is about the
`.encrypted`-marker directory mismatch and BUDGET_DATA_DIR, not about
Ensure/os.ReadFile at all.

But the material fact controls the ruling. This exact citation and rationale
did not originate with the worker:

  Issue #26 body (`gh issue view 26 --json body`): "The reason
  `Snapshotter.Ensure` reads the source bytes rather than using `os.Link` is
  specifically the unreadable case: `docs/whatif-mcp-followups-2026-08-09.md`
  §3 records that this MCP detects encryption in the wrong directory, so a
  blind copy of ciphertext would appear to succeed and the caller's
  abort-before-write guarantee would silently not fire."

  .swarm/X-RUN-SPEC.md X1 (the dispatch brief itself, lines 54-57): "The
  reason `Snapshotter.Ensure` reads bytes instead of `os.Link` is the
  UNREADABLE case (docs/whatif-mcp-followups-2026-08-09.md §3: blind copy of
  ciphertext would appear to succeed and the abort-before-write guarantee
  would silently not fire)."

  The test's comment on disk is a close paraphrase of both, reproducing the
  same claim and the same citation almost verbatim. The worker did not
  fabricate this provenance; it inherited it faithfully from the material it
  was dispatched with. If the citation is wrong, the error was authored
  upstream of the worker (issue #26, echoed into the SPEC's own task
  description) and the worker's fidelity to its brief is not a standards
  violation attributable to this work product.

On the two questions posed: (1) the WRITTEN standard for X1 — the four-item
acceptance list — nowhere requires the new test's comment to carry a
correct documentary citation; citation accuracy is not one of the
enumerated criteria and all four enumerated criteria are met. (2) The house
norm that doc comments must match what the code does (seen repeatedly in
this repo's review specs, e.g. REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC S5, REVIEW_FIXES_SPEC R6)
is aimed at comments asserting something about the code's own behavior that
a future reader would otherwise have to rediscover by hand — classically a
function's own godoc. Here the defective sentence is explanatory motivation
for a test, lifted near-verbatim from the issue/spec that commissioned it;
the defect is located in that upstream text, not invented or embellished by
the worker's own judgment. checker-second's report agrees the test "would
have been sound on its own steam citing Ensure's actual
missing-file-must-abort guarantee... It did not need the encryption tie-in
at all" — i.e. the finding is about an avoidable citation error in
inherited framing, not a defect in test construction or in honesty about
what the test checks (which was the actual subject of issue #26 and the
actual subject of X1's acceptance criteria).

Sending this back to the worker for a comment-wording fix, when all four
acceptance criteria are met and mutation-verified and the wrong citation
was handed to the worker by the brief itself, would penalize the work for a
defect in the constitution's own supporting material rather than in the
work. That is a correction to route to the lead (fix the SPEC/issue
citation, or have the worker reword the comment to cite Ensure's actual
missing-source-must-abort guarantee as checker-second suggests), not a
task-blocking standards failure at this attempt.

VERDICT: OVERRULE — the FAIL is set aside; the work meets the written X1
acceptance criteria at attempt 2. Recommend the lead separately correct the
§3 citation (in the test comment and, upstream, in X-RUN-SPEC.md/issue #26)
as a non-blocking follow-up.
